  "before_interfaces": {
    "lem-qftx-photon-invariant-measure-isometry": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Countable Choice for the Hilbert-space supplier. Let $M=\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$, let $\\omega(\\mathbf k)=|\\mathbf k|$, and equip $M$ with the measure $d\\nu(\\mathbf k)=d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\omega(\\mathbf k))$. On $\\mathbb C^3$ use the positive Hermitian pairing $\\overline u\\cdot v=\\sum_{j=1}^3\\overline{u_j}v_j$ and the complex-bilinear extension of the Euclidean dot product in the transversality condition. Define $\\mathcal H_T^\\nu=\\{f\\in L^2(M,\\nu;\\mathbb C^3):\\mathbf k\\cdot f(\\mathbf k)=0\\text{ for }\\nu\\text{-almost every }\\mathbf k\\}$. Let $\\mathcal H_T^0=\\{u\\in L^2(M,d^3\\mathbf k;\\mathbb C^3):\\mathbf k\\cdot u(\\mathbf k)=0\\text{ for Lebesgue-almost every }\\mathbf k\\}$ with the second-variable-linear integral pairing. Then $Wf(\\mathbf k)=f(\\mathbf k)/\\sqrt{2\\omega(\\mathbf k)}$ is a unitary map from $\\mathcal H_T^\\nu$ onto $\\mathcal H_T^0$."
    ],
    "def-qftx-photon-helicity-bundle": [
      null,
      "## Definition\n\nAssume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Put $M=\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$, $\\omega(\\mathbf k)=|\\mathbf k|$, and $d\\nu(\\mathbf k)=d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\omega(\\mathbf k))$. Let $d\\Omega$ denote polar surface measure on $S^2$. Polar coordinates give $d\\nu=(r/2)\\,dr\\,d\\Omega$, and every orthogonal map preserves $\\nu$. The transverse one-photon Hilbert space $\\mathcal H_T^\\nu$ consists of the equivalence classes $f\\in L^2(M,\\nu;\\mathbb C^3)$ satisfying $\\mathbf k\\cdot f(\\mathbf k)=0$ for $\\nu$-almost every $\\mathbf k$, with pairing $\\langle f,g\\rangle_\\nu=\\int_M\\overline{f(\\mathbf k)}\\cdot g(\\mathbf k)\\,d\\nu(\\mathbf k)$, linear in $g$. The preceding measure-isometry lemma gives the unitary $Wf(\\mathbf k)=f(\\mathbf k)/\\sqrt{2\\omega(\\mathbf k)}$ from this space to the transverse space defined with measure $d^3\\mathbf k$. The null set $\\mathbf k=0$ is omitted throughout.\n\nFor $\\mathbf n\\in S^2$, define $P(\\mathbf n)=I-\\mathbf n\\mathbf n^T$. Extend the real cross product complex-linearly by $(\\mathbf n\\times v)_i=\\sum_{j,\\ell=1}^3\\epsilon_{ij\\ell}n_jv_\\ell$, where $\\epsilon_{ij\\ell}$ is totally antisymmetric and $\\epsilon_{123}=1$, and set $h(\\mathbf n)=i\\,\\mathbf n\\times$ and $P_\\sigma(\\mathbf n)=(P(\\mathbf n)+\\sigma h(\\mathbf n))/2$ for $\\sigma\\in\\{+1,-1\\}$. Each $P_\\sigma(\\mathbf n)$ is a rank-one orthogonal projector on the complex transverse plane $P(\\mathbf n)\\mathbb C^3$, and $h$ has eigenvalue $\\sigma$ on its range. The fibers $\\mathcal L_{\\sigma,\\mathbf n}=\\operatorname{ran}P_\\sigma(\\mathbf n)$ form smooth complex line subbundles of $S^2\\times\\mathbb C^3$. Here a smooth line subbundle means a family of one-dimensional complex fibers with smooth local unit sections. The common smooth domain for differential calculations is\n\n$$\\mathcal D=\\{f\\in C_c^\\infty(M;\\mathbb C^3):\\mathbf k\\cdot f(\\mathbf k)=0\\text{ for every }\\mathbf k\\in M\\}.$$\n\nIt is dense in $\\mathcal H_T^\\nu$. For $0<r_0<r_1<\\infty$, the mass-shell measure of the radial annulus is $\\nu(\\{r_0\\le|\\mathbf k|\\le r_1\\})=\\pi(r_1^2-r_0^2)$; in particular $\\nu$ is sigma-finite."
    ],
    "thm-qftx-photon-rotations-helicity": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice, let $\\hbar>0$, and use the transverse Hilbert space, helicity projectors, and dense domain $\\mathcal D$ defined above. Let $e_1,e_2,e_3$ be the standard coordinate basis of $\\mathbb R^3$. Let $SO(3)=\\{R\\in M_3(\\mathbb R):R^TR=I,\\det R=1\\}$ with the topology inherited from the Frobenius matrix norm $\\|A\\|_F=(\\sum_{i,j=1}^3|A_{ij}|^2)^{1/2}$. For $R\\in SO(3)$ define $U(R)f(\\mathbf k)=R f(R^{-1}\\mathbf k)$. Then $U$ is a strongly continuous unitary representation on $\\mathcal H_T^\\nu$, and $P_\\sigma(\\mathbf k/|\\mathbf k|)\\,U(R)=U(R)\\,P_\\sigma(\\mathbf k/|\\mathbf k|)$ for $\\sigma=\\pm1$. For a unit axis $\\mathbf a$, put $R_{\\mathbf a}(t)=\\exp(t[\\mathbf a]_\\times)$, where $[\\mathbf a]_\\times v=\\mathbf a\\times v$, and define $(S_{\\mathbf a})_{ij}=-i\\sum_{\\ell=1}^3a_\\ell\\epsilon_{\\ell ij}$, $L_{\\mathbf a}=-i\\hbar\\,(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf k)\\cdot\\nabla_{\\mathbf k}$, and $S_{\\mathbf a}^{\\mathrm{op}}=\\hbar S_{\\mathbf a}$. The unique self-adjoint Stone generator $J_{\\mathbf a}$ specified by $U(R_{\\mathbf a}(t))=\\exp(-itJ_{\\mathbf a}/\\hbar)$ acts on $\\mathcal D$ as $J_{\\mathbf a}=L_{\\mathbf a}+S_{\\mathbf a}^{\\mathrm{op}}$. This total generator preserves transversality, while its orbital and spin terms generally do not separately preserve the transverse space. The stated self-adjoint operators are the Stone generators; the differential expression is their restriction to $\\mathcal D$."
    ],
    "prop-qftx-helicity-chern-obstruction": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nOrient $S^2$ outward, so its area form is $d\\Omega=\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta\\wedge d\\varphi$. In the spherical chart away from the poles let $\\mathbf n=(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\varphi,\\sin\\theta\\sin\\varphi,\\cos\\theta)$, $\\mathbf e_\\theta=(\\cos\\theta\\cos\\varphi,\\cos\\theta\\sin\\varphi,-\\sin\\theta)$, and $\\mathbf e_\\varphi=(-\\sin\\varphi,\\cos\\varphi,0)$. For $\\sigma\\in\\{+1,-1\\}$ set $e_\\sigma=(\\mathbf e_\\theta+i\\sigma\\mathbf e_\\varphi)/\\sqrt2$, a local unit frame of the helicity line $\\mathcal L_{\\sigma,\\mathbf n}$. With Hermitian pairing $\\overline u\\cdot v$, define the skew-Hermitian connection form $\\omega_\\sigma=e_\\sigma^\\dagger de_\\sigma$, its curvature $\\Omega_\\sigma=d\\omega_\\sigma$, the real Berry connection $A_\\sigma=-i e_\\sigma^\\dagger de_\\sigma$, and $F_\\sigma=dA_\\sigma$. Then\n$$A_\\sigma=-\\sigma\\cos\\theta\\,d\\varphi,\\qquad F_\\sigma=\\sigma\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta\\wedge d\\varphi,\\qquad \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_{S^2}F_\\sigma=2\\sigma.$$\nUse the Chern-Weil convention $c_1(\\mathcal L_\\sigma)[S^2]=\\frac{i}{2\\pi}\\int_{S^2}\\Omega_\\sigma$ for $\\nabla=d+\\omega_\\sigma$; then $c_1(\\mathcal L_\\sigma)[S^2]=-2\\sigma$. The north and south unit gauges $e_\\sigma^N=e^{i\\sigma\\varphi}e_\\sigma$ and $e_\\sigma^S=e^{-i\\sigma\\varphi}e_\\sigma$ have transition $e_\\sigma^N=e^{2i\\sigma\\varphi}e_\\sigma^S$, of winding $2\\sigma$ as $\\varphi$ increases from $0$ to $2\\pi$. Thus neither helicity line admits a global smooth unit section. This obstruction concerns each line separately and makes no nontriviality claim about the rank-two transverse complex bundle."
    ],
    "cex-qftx-fixed-polarization-tensor-factor": [
      null
    ]
  }
}
